  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Today, periods of rain and high elevated\nsnowfall will move across central and northern Nevada through\nTuesday morning as a Pacific upper trough moves further inland. \nThe valleys are expected to get rain as snow levels will be above \n7000 feet, but by the evening and overnight, snow levels will \ngradually fall as colder temperatures move in, dropping to below \n5000 feet across the region. Snow amounts in the valleys are \nexpected to be light just below half an inch while some higher\nelevated valleys above 6000 feet, in particular near Ely, could\nsee up to an inch or more. High passes and summits in northern \nNevada have a 35-55% chance of seeing up to one inch or more of \nsnow, with central Nevada passes and summits seeing a 30-60% \nchance of two inches or more. High temperatures expected to be \nnear normal today with highs in the 50s (40s in Humboldt County), \nand then drop to just a few degrees below normal on Tuesday from \nthe cold front with highs in the low 40s across the region. \nElevated southerly winds in central Nevada with speeds up to 10-15\nmph, gusts as high as 25-35 mph today. Winds on Tuesday will be \nlighter but still breezy with gusts up to 15-20 mph.\n\nShortly after the first weather system move out of Nevada by\nTuesday night, another system will impact Nevada early Wednesday\nmorning with additional periods of rain and snow which is expected\nto last until Friday morning. Snow levels will stay just around\n5000 feet as temperatures continue on a cooling trend, with\nchances valleys seeing rain snow mix until Thursday night when\nsnow levels drop below 4000 feet. The cooling temperature trend \nwill keep temperatures just a few degrees below normal in the 30s \nto 40s Wednesday, then down into the 30s across central and \nnorthern Nevada Thursday and Friday. Overnight lows will also see \na cooling trend dropping into the teens to 20s each night, with \nFriday night seeing temperatures in the single digits. Breezy\nsoutherly winds Wednesday with the passing front, becoming\ngenerally light Thursday and Friday as the front passes.\n\nConditions will dry out by Friday afternoon and over the weekend,\ngiving a break from the week long weather. Temperatures will see a\ngradual rise, but stay below normal with highs in the 30s as\nclouds stay over Nevada due to another low pressure system to the\nnorthwest. Models have this system staying over the northwest and\nnot entering Nevada, but chances of precipitation entering over \nnorthwestern Nevada cannot be ruled out.",
